<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.12.2: Writing an Inequality to Represent a Constraint</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00585</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.12.2: Writing an Inequality to Represent a Constraint</md:title>
    <md:uuid>99539b32-fe44-47b0-ad49-c171b7466d4e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">For questions 1 – 8, use the following scenario:</para>
      <quote id="quote-00001">The homeowner is worried about the work needed to maintain a grass lawn and flower
        beds, so she is now looking at some low-maintenance materials.</quote>
        <quote id="quote-00002">She is
        considering a combination of the materials shown to cover the yard. Her budget is still
        $3,000.</quote>
        <quote id="quote-00003">
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item>artificial turf: $15 per square foot</item>
            <item>gravel: $3 per square foot</item>
          </list>
        </quote>
        <quote id="quote-00004">Here is a graph representing some constraints in this situation.</quote>
        <quote id="quote-00005">
          
            <media alt="insert alt text">
              <image mime-type="svg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_12_02_01.svg"/>
            </media>
          
        </quote>

      <!--Q#1-->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00003"> The graph shows a line going through <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>500</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>100</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. In this situation, what does
            the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>500</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>100</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> mean?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00006">The cost of installing 500 square feet of gravel and 100 square feet of artificial
            turf is $3000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007"> Write an equation that the line represents.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00004"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3000</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00011"> What do the solutions to the equation mean?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00014">The solutions are all combinations of square feet of gravel and artificial turf that
            would cost $3000 to install.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q4-->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00015"> The point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>600</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>200</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is located to the right and above the line. Does that
            combination of turf and gravel meet the homeowner's constraints? Be prepared to show
            your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00018">No. Answers may vary, but here is a sample. If she installs 600 square feet of
            gravel and 200 square feet of turf, the cost is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>600</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>200</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>, which is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1800</m:mn><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>000</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> or <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>$</m:mi><m:mn>4800</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q5-->
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00019"> Choose another point in the same region (to the right and above the line). Check if
            the combination meets the homeowner's constraints.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00022">Answers may vary, but here is a sample. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>900</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>50</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. This combination would cost
            more than $3000 to install.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q6-->
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00023"> The point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>200</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>100</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> is located to the left and below the line. Does that
            combination of turf and gravel meet the homeowner's constraints? Be prepared to show
            your reasoning.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00025">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00026">Yes. Answers may vary, but here is a sample: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>200</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>100</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2100</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. This
            amount is less than $3000.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00027"> Choose another point in the same region (to the left and below the line). Check if
            the combination meets the homeowner's constraints.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00029">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00030">Answers may vary, but here is a sample: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>600</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>25</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. This combination would cost
            under $3000 to install.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#8-->

      <exercise id="exer-00008">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00031"> Write an inequality that represents the constraints in this situation. Explain what
            the solutions mean and show the solution region on the graph.</para>
          <para id="para-00032"></para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00033">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00034">Answers may vary, but here is a sample:</para>
          <para id="para-00035"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>3000</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>. The solutions represent all possible combinations of square
            feet of gravel and turf that would cost no more than $3000 to install.</para>
          
            <media alt="The graph of an inequality in the first quadrant of the coordinate plane. The x-axis label indicates it represents the squre feet of gravel sod. The y-axis label says it represents the square feet of artificial turf.">
              <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_14_03_01.svg"/>
            </media>
          
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>

      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Video: Writing an Inequality to Represent a Constraint</title>
      <para id="para-00036">Watch the following video to learn more about representing constraints using
        inequalities.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ffb9464e95524398270ca405c11a6064703c8cd0"/>
      </media>
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00003">
      <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
        <exercise id="exer-00010">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
          <para id="para-00010"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_2_12_2_SC"/></para>
          </problem>
          </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00005">
        <title>Writing Inequalities to Represent Constraints</title>
        <para id="para-00038"><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
        <para id="para-00039">Elena needs to earn at least $450 a week during her summer break to pay for college.
          She works two jobs. One as a swimming instructor that pays $9 an hour and the other as an
          intern in a genetics lab for $22.50 per hour. How many hours does Elena need to work at
          each job to earn at least $450 per week?</para>
        <para id="para-00040">Let x be the number of hours she works teaching swimming and let y be the number of
          hours she works as an intern.</para>
        <para id="para-00041">Write an inequality that would model this situation.</para>
        <para id="para-00042">She earns $9 per hour at the job as a swimming instructor and $22.50 in the genetics
          lab. At each job the number of hours multiplied by the hourly wage will give the amount
          earned at that job.</para>
        <para id="para-00043">
          
            <media alt="Mathematical expression: 9 times x plus 22.5 times y is greater than or equal to 450. Above the inequality, labels indicate the term representing 9 times x is the amount earned as a swimming instructor, and the term representing 22.5 times y is the amount earned in the genetics lab. The phrase &quot;is at least 450 dollars&quot; represents the label for greater than or equal to 450.">
              <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_14_05_01.jpg"/>
            </media>
          
       </para>

        <note class="try numbered-exercises" id="note-00002">
        <title>Try It: Writing Inequalities to Represent Constraints</title>
        <para id="para-00044">A baker sells pies and cakes at the bakery. Each pie sells for $14 and each cake sells
          for $23. Between all the sales, the baker wants to earn at least $525. How many pies and
          cakes does the baker need to sell to meet their goal?</para>
        <!--Text
        Entry Interaction Start -->
        <exercise id="exer-00009">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00045">Let <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> be the number of pies sold and let c be the number of cakes sold. Write
              an inequality that would model this situation.</para>

          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00046">Compare your answer: You answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
            <para id="para-00008"><m:math><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mn>23</m:mn><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mspace width="0.278em"/><m:mn>525</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
        </exercise>
        </note>
        <!--Interaction
        End -->
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
